<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shammar Yahrish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 275–310 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shammar Yahrish was a king of Himyar in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in the late 3rd and early 4th centuries CE. He absorbed the Kingdom of Saba around 275 and conquered the Kingdom of Hadramawt around 300, uniting south Arabia under Himyarite rule for the first time. His inscriptions style him king of Saba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhu-raydan">Dhu Raydan</a>, Hadramawt and Yamnat.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 275 CE King Shammar Yuhar'ish (<b>Shammar Yahrish</b>) annexed Saba and, around 300, the Kingdom of Hadramawt, uniting all of south Arabia under one crown for the first time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 275 CE, however, the Himyarite king <b>Shammar Yuhar’ish</b> brought Saba under his rule and later added Hadramawt, unifying south-western Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 3rd century CE Saba sacked Shabwa, and around 300 CE the Himyarite Kingdom under <b>Shammar Yahrish</b> conquered Hadramawt outright.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Kingdom of Hadramawt</a></li></ul></section>
